<com:TContent ID="body" >
<h1>Testing and Continuous Integration</h1>

<p class="block-content">
PRADO carries its own test suites. They live in the <tt>tests</tt> directory of the
<tt>pradosoft/prado</tt> package, and Composer installs that directory together with the
framework. PHPUnit and PHPStan cover the PHP source. Vitest and Playwright cover the
client-side scripts and the pages those scripts drive. A GitHub Actions workflow runs all
of them on every push and pull request.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The suites need the development dependencies, which the published package does not install.
Clone the framework repository and run <tt>composer devtools</tt> to get a complete
development setup.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
git clone https://github.com/pradosoft/prado
cd prado
composer devtools
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>composer devtools</tt> performs four steps. It updates the Composer dependencies.
It installs the npm packages. It downloads the Playwright browsers. It clones
<tt>prado-demos</tt> into <tt>vendor/pradosoft/prado-demos</tt>, which the functional
tests drive.
</p>

<h2>The tools</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Each tool keeps its configuration in a file at the repository root, and each has a Composer
script that runs it with the right arguments.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Covers</th><th>Configuration</th><th>Command</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td>PHPUnit</td>
	<td>PHP unit tests</td>
	<td><tt>phpunit.xml</tt></td>
	<td><tt>composer unittest</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>PHPStan</td>
	<td>PHP static analysis</td>
	<td><tt>phpstan.neon.dist</tt></td>
	<td><tt>composer stan</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>PHP-CS-Fixer</td>
	<td>PHP coding standard</td>
	<td><tt>.php-cs-fixer.dist.php</tt></td>
	<td><tt>composer fix</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Vitest</td>
	<td>JavaScript unit tests</td>
	<td><tt>vitest.config.js</tt></td>
	<td><tt>composer jstest</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>ESLint</td>
	<td>JavaScript lint</td>
	<td><tt>eslint.config.js</tt></td>
	<td><tt>composer jslint</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Playwright</td>
	<td>Browser functional tests</td>
	<td><tt>playwright.config.js</tt></td>
	<td><tt>composer functionaltest</tt></td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
Two commands chain the others. <tt>composer fulltest</tt> runs PHP-CS-Fixer, then PHPStan,
then the unit tests. <tt>composer jsfull</tt> runs the ESLint fixer and then the JavaScript
tests.
</p>

<h2>PHPUnit</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The PHP tests live under <tt>tests/unit</tt> and end in <tt>Test.php</tt>. <tt>phpunit.xml</tt>
bootstraps them through <tt>tests/test_tools/phpunit_bootstrap.php</tt>, which constructs a
<tt>TApplication</tt> without running it so that every test has an application singleton
available. The bootstrap defines the constant <tt>PRADO_TEST_RUN</tt>. Each run writes a
testdox report to <tt>build/tests/index.html</tt>.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>phpunit.xml</tt> declares four named suites.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Suite</th><th>Contains</th><th>Command</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>unit</tt></td>
	<td>Everything under <tt>tests/unit</tt> except the PHPStan extension tests.</td>
	<td><tt>composer unittest</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>phpstan</tt></td>
	<td>The tests that verify the PHPStan extensions against fixture files.</td>
	<td><tt>composer stantest</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>selenium</tt></td>
	<td>The Selenium functional tests under <tt>tests/FunctionalTests</tt>.</td>
	<td><tt>composer seleniumtest</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>functional</tt></td>
	<td>Superseded by Playwright. Run the Playwright suite instead.</td>
	<td><tt>composer functionaltest</tt></td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Database tests</h3>

<p class="block-content">
The data-layer tests run against real database servers. The <tt>tests</tt> directory holds
one schema script per server, named <tt>initdb_mysql.sql</tt>, <tt>initdb_pgsql.sql</tt>,
<tt>initdb_mssql.sql</tt>, <tt>initdb_oracle.sql</tt>, <tt>initdb_firebird.sql</tt> and
<tt>initdb_ibm.sql</tt>. Load the script into a database named <tt>prado_unitest</tt> before
running the suite.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
A developer without those servers sets the environment variable
<tt>PRADO_UNITTEST_SKIP_DB=1</tt>. The variable appends a marker to each connection error, so
a missing server reads as expected rather than as a regression. A test calls
<tt>PradoUnit::skipDatabaseTests()</tt> to decide whether to skip itself.
</p>

<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<h3>PradoUnit and TTestApplication</h3>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>PradoUnit</tt> is the shared helper class for the suite, at
<tt>tests/unit/PradoUnit.php</tt>. It reads and writes instance and static properties at
every level of a class hierarchy, including private properties declared in ancestors. That
lets a test snapshot global state such as <tt>Prado::$_aliases</tt> before it mutates it and
restore the state afterwards. It also opens the driver-specific connections the data-layer
tests use, and classifies a connection failure as a missing server, a missing database, or a
missing table.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TTestApplication</tt> extends <tt>TApplication</tt> for use inside a test. Its base path
defaults to the system temporary directory, and it resolves paths without looking for an
application configuration file, so a bare <tt>new TTestApplication()</tt> works with no setup.
Its <tt>exit()</tt> records the exit code in <tt>capturedExitCode</tt> instead of ending the
process, which lets a test assert on a <tt>TExitException</tt> path. <tt>restoreApplication()</tt>
puts the global state back and belongs in <tt>tearDown()</tt>.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Neither class carries a namespace, and neither is autoloaded. Requiring
<tt>PradoUnit.php</tt> loads both.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
require 'vendor/pradosoft/prado/tests/unit/PradoUnit.php';

class MyTest extends PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase
{
    private TTestApplication $app;

    protected function setUp(): void
    {
        $this-&gt;app = new TTestApplication();
    }

    protected function tearDown(): void
    {
        $this-&gt;app-&gt;restoreApplication();
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>PHPStan</h2>

<p class="block-content">
PHPStan analyzes the <tt>framework</tt> directory at level 2, targeting PHP 8.3.
<tt>composer stan</tt> runs the analysis with a 512 MB memory limit.
</p>

<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.2" />
<h3>Extensions for the dynamic API</h3>

<p class="block-content">
PRADO resolves many method and property names at run time, which a static analyzer cannot
see. A plain PHPStan run therefore reports a component's virtual properties and its
<tt>dy</tt> and <tt>fx</tt> methods as undefined. The framework answers that with its own
extensions under <tt>framework/PHPStan</tt>, registered in the <tt>services</tt> section of
<tt>phpstan.neon.dist</tt>.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Extension</th><th>Teaches PHPStan</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>DynamicMethodsClassReflectionExtension</tt></td>
	<td>Any method name on a <tt>TComponent</tt> starting with <tt>dy</tt> or <tt>fx</tt> is a valid call.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TComponentPropertiesReflectionExtension</tt></td>
	<td>Every <tt>getX()</tt> and <tt>setX()</tt> pair is the virtual property <tt>X</tt>, typed from the accessor.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TComponentHasMethodTypeSpecifyingExtension</tt></td>
	<td>A true <tt>hasMethod()</tt> guard narrows the subject so the guarded call is valid.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TComponentCanGetPropertyTypeSpecifyingExtension</tt></td>
	<td>A true <tt>canGetProperty()</tt> guard admits the getter and the property read.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TComponentCanSetPropertyTypeSpecifyingExtension</tt></td>
	<td>A true <tt>canSetProperty()</tt> guard admits the setter and the property write.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TComponentIsaTypeSpecifyingExtension</tt></td>
	<td><tt>isa()</tt> narrows a type the way <tt>instanceof</tt> does, across interfaces and class constants.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>PradoMethodVisibleStaticMethodTypeSpecifyingExtension</tt></td>
	<td>A true <tt>Prado::method_visible()</tt> guard narrows the subject to have that method.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
The extensions have tests of their own under <tt>tests/unit/PHPStan</tt>, which
<tt>composer stantest</tt> runs. Each extension gets a pair of cases against a fixture file.
One case runs PHPStan without the extensions and requires errors. The other runs it with the
extensions and requires none.
</p>

<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<h2>Vitest</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The <a href="?page=Advanced.Scripts">client-side scripts</a> under
<tt>framework/Web/Javascripts/source</tt> have unit tests under
<tt>tests/js</tt>, in files ending in <tt>.test.js</tt>. Vitest runs them in a jsdom
environment, so a test gets a real <tt>document</tt> and <tt>window</tt> for jQuery to work
against. The <tt>globals</tt> option injects <tt>describe</tt>, <tt>it</tt> and
<tt>expect</tt>, which matches the Jest API.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
composer jstest     # one run
composer jswatch    # re-run on change
composer jslint     # ESLint over the source scripts
composer jsfix      # ESLint with --fix
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The framework scripts load as plain <tt>script</tt> tags rather than as modules, so a test
cannot import them directly. Each test imports from an adapter under
<tt>tests/js/adapters</tt> instead, and the adapter is the only file that knows how the
source is loaded. Converting a script to an ES module changes its adapter and leaves the
tests alone.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
ESLint reads the same source directory. Its configuration declares the PRADO globals,
including <tt>Prado</tt>, <tt>Logger</tt> and <tt>jQuery</tt>. Rules that the legacy code
breaks widely, such as <tt>no-undef</tt> and <tt>no-unused-vars</tt>, are set to warn so the
lint report surfaces them without failing the build.
</p>

<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<h2>Playwright</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The functional tests drive a real browser against real pages. They live under
<tt>tests/playwright</tt> in files ending in <tt>.spec.js</tt>. Playwright starts a PHP
built-in server on <tt>127.0.0.1:8037</tt> for the run and stops it afterwards, so nothing
needs to be running first. The server serves the whole repository tree, and the two groups of
tests reach their pages through different URL prefixes.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Directory</th><th>Drives</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>active-controls</tt></td>
	<td>The AJAX active controls, under <tt>tests/harness</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>validators</tt></td>
	<td>The validation controls, under <tt>tests/harness</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>features</tt></td>
	<td>Individual framework features, under <tt>tests/harness</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>tickets</tt></td>
	<td>Regression pages for closed tickets, under <tt>tests/harness</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>issues</tt></td>
	<td>Regression pages for closed issues, under <tt>tests/harness</tt>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>quickstart</tt></td>
	<td>The quickstart sample pages, under <tt>vendor/pradosoft/prado-demos</tt>.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
The quickstart specs need the demo applications, which <tt>tests/setup-demos.sh</tt> installs.
The script clones <tt>prado-demos</tt> into <tt>vendor/pradosoft/prado-demos</tt>, installs its
own dependencies, and then replaces its copy of <tt>pradosoft/prado</tt> with a symlink to the
working tree. The demos therefore run against the code under test. Both functional test
commands call the script before Playwright starts.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
composer functest                       # Chromium only
composer functionaltest                 # Chromium, Firefox and WebKit
npx playwright test --project=firefox   # one browser
npx playwright test active-controls     # one directory
npm run playwright:headed               # watch the browser work
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
The run is deliberately sequential. One worker executes the specs in order and no test is
retried, which reproduces the behavior of the Selenium suite that these tests replaced. A
failing test keeps a screenshot and a video. The HTML report lands in
<tt>build/playwright-report</tt> and the raw artifacts in <tt>build/playwright-results</tt>.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Each spec wraps its Playwright page in <tt>PradoTestHelper</tt> from
<tt>tests/playwright/helpers.js</tt>. The helper exposes the method names the PHP Selenium
base class used, such as <tt>byId()</tt>, <tt>assertText()</tt> and <tt>waitForAjaxCalls()</tt>,
so a converted spec reads like its original. The Selenium suite still ships under
<tt>tests/FunctionalTests</tt>. <tt>composer seleniumtest</tt> runs it, which needs
<tt>geckodriver</tt> on port 4444.
</p>

<h2>Continuous integration</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The workflow at <tt>.github/workflows/prado.yml</tt> runs on every push and every pull
request. It is split into jobs that run in parallel. Each matrix sets <tt>fail-fast</tt> to
off, so one broken combination does not cancel the others. The jobs that do not sweep a
matrix pin PHP to
the version in the workflow's <tt>PHP_VERSION</tt> variable. The Node version comes from
<tt>.nvmrc</tt>.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Job</th><th>Runs on</th><th>Does</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td>Prado</td>
	<td>Ubuntu, PHP 8.1, 8.2 and 8.3</td>
	<td>Validates <tt>composer.json</tt>, checks the coding standard, runs PHPStan, runs the unit tests, and runs the Selenium functional tests. MySQL, PostgreSQL and Memcached are provided as services.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Prado JS</td>
	<td>Ubuntu, Node from <tt>.nvmrc</tt></td>
	<td>Runs ESLint and the Vitest suite.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Prado Playwright</td>
	<td>Ubuntu, one job per browser</td>
	<td>Installs the browser, sets up the demos, and runs the Playwright suite. The HTML report is uploaded as an artifact and kept for 30 days.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Prado OS (macOS)</td>
	<td>macOS</td>
	<td>Checks the coding standard, runs PHPStan, and runs the unit tests against Homebrew MySQL, PostgreSQL and Memcached.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Prado OS (Windows)</td>
	<td>Windows</td>
	<td>The same checks against MySQL and PostgreSQL. The Memcached extension is omitted because libmemcached is unavailable there.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Prado Data</td>
	<td>Ubuntu, one job per server</td>
	<td>Loads the schema into a containerized Microsoft SQL Server, Firebird, Oracle or IBM DB2, then runs the matching suite under <tt>tests/unit/Data/DbSpecific</tt>.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
The coding-standard step reports through <tt>cs2pr</tt> and the PHPStan step does the same, so
a violation appears as an annotation on the changed line in the pull request rather than only
in the log.
</p>

<h2>Testing an application</h2>

<p class="block-content">
An application tests itself with PHPUnit directly, and needs nothing from the framework to do
it. The framework's helpers are useful when a test has to construct an application object.
The published package includes the <tt>tests</tt> directory, so
<tt>vendor/pradosoft/prado/tests/unit/PradoUnit.php</tt> is present after a normal install.
Requiring it makes <tt>PradoUnit</tt> and <tt>TTestApplication</tt> available, as shown above.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
For an end-to-end test of an application's pages, use Playwright against the application's own
web server. The quickstart specs under <tt>tests/playwright/quickstart</tt> are a worked
example of driving a PRADO application that way.
</p>

</com:TContent>
